INT. FERRY - CALL CENTER DECK - DAY

A converted car deck: forty cubicles under fluorescent tubes, water slapping the hull. A fire drill siren WAILS.

Nobody has stood up.

At the central desk, PRUDENCE KWAN, 40s, floor manager, headset around her neck, scrolls on her laptop. In the next cubicle, GARRETT LINDQVIST, 30s, a lanyard of seventeen badges, types with one finger. Behind them, DAPHNE ROUMELIOTIS, 50s, QA lead, eats a banana.

PRUDENCE
It's a drill. They said it's a drill.

GARRETT
Who said?

DAPHNE
Facilities.

GARRETT
We don't have Facilities. We're on a boat.

PRUDENCE
She means the Captain's office.

DAPHNE
I mean Facilities.

The siren wails on.

GARRETT
We should evacuate.

PRUDENCE
To where? We're in the middle of the Sound.

DAPHNE
The muster point is the lifeboat.

GARRETT
Which one is the lifeboat?

DAPHNE
The one with the sign.

PRUDENCE
The sign fell off in March.

GARRETT
I filed a ticket.

DAPHNE
Garrett, you don't have a ticket system.

GARRETT
That's why it's still open.

The siren stops. In the silence, the three of them look at each other. Nobody has moved.

PRUDENCE
Okay, back to work.

DAPHNE
Agreed.

GARRETT
Agreed.

They sit. Headsets on. A phone rings.

PRUDENCE
(into headset)
Thank you for calling, how can I help?

Behind them, the ferry's horn sounds, long and low. They don't look up.
